% Summary: Tokenizer comparison for Telugu (agglutinative), Hindi, and
% English using small BERT models. Releases a Telugu gold morpheme
% segmentation dataset (600 derivational + 7000 inflectional forms).
% Headline finding is about downstream task performance, not directly
% alignment of segmentations: Unigram beats BPE on most downstream
% tasks for Telugu, and morphological alignment correlates moderately
% but is a weaker driver than algorithm choice. Useful as recent
% multilingual corroboration that the BPE/Unigram split persists for
% morphologically rich languages.
% Verbatim from the paper:
% "the choice of tokenizer algorithm is the most significant factor
%  influencing performance, with Unigram-based tokenizers consistently
%  outperforming BPE across most settings"
% "while better morphological alignment shows a moderate, positive
%  correlation with performance on text classification and structure
%  prediction tasks, its impact is secondary to the tokenizer algorithm"
@inproceedings{vemula-etal-2025-rethinking,
    title = "Rethinking Tokenization for Rich Morphology: The Dominance of {U}nigram over {BPE} and Morphological Alignment",
